[Music]  hi there when you're doing a reading  exam how you read the text is very  different to how you would read a  magazine or a book in this video we're  going to have a look at four ways to  help you get the best marks you can in a  reading exam let's have a look at how  you read the text first the first time  you read the text read it quickly from  start to finish to get a basic idea of  what the whole text is about then read  the questions when you've understood the  questions go back and read the text  carefully this time look for key  information like names places dates  numbers etc sometimes the words from the  questions or synonyms may be used in the  text look for synonyms or related words  to the question and circle the key  information this will then help you when  you read the questions again and start  to find the places in the text where the  answers are you may need to read some  parts of the text two or three times to  make sure you get the right answers now  let's have a look at difficult questions  in a reading exam you'll normally find  one or two tricky questions the  important thing to do here is not to  panic if you can't answer a question  straight away don't worry leave it and  go back to it at the end don't waste too  much time on difficult questions you  can't answer it's better to focus on the  questions you can answer and go back to  these difficult questions at the end  once you know the questions you need to  find the answers in the text they're  often well hidden but the questions are  normally in chronological order this  means the first question will refer to  the beginning of the text the second to  the next part and so forth  it's a good idea to underline or  highlight key words to help you focus  and find the answers  finally let's think about what you can  do if there are difficult words in the  text that you don't know sometimes you  may not even need to understand these  words to understand or answer the  question if you really don't know a word  and you do need it to answer the  question see if you can use the context  to help you guess the meaning read the  sentences before and after the sentence  you're focusing on to help you with the  context it will also help you if you can  see what type of word it is is it a noun  an adjective and adverb does the word  have a positive or negative meaning also  think about the topic of the text which  words do you know related to the topic  could it be a synonym or is the word  made of words you already know but for  example peanut butter from your  understanding of the words peanut and  butter you can assume that it's like  butter  but made of peanuts  [Music]  for more tips on learning English and  preparing for your English exams  subscribe to our Channel and visit the  learn English teens website where you  will find loads more learning material  [Music]

